  const JADEN = {
    1: '> Jaden got a text from his boss: "Can you come in at 4? Reply by 10." He read it and typed "Yes."\n\n> He read a food label. It said **contains peanuts**. His cousin is allergic.\n\n> At work, a checklist was on the wall: wipe counters, check the fridge, log it, initial it. Jaden did every step.\n\n> At home, he read a letter for his mom. It said: reply within **14 days**. He wrote the date on the calendar.',
    2: '> Jaden\'s phone buzzed at 6:40 a.m. A text from his shift supervisor: "Can u come in at 4 instead of 5? Reply by 10." He read it twice, typed "Yes, see you at 4," and checked the bus app. The 32B was running eight minutes late, so he grabbed a granola bar and read the label while he waited: 190 calories, contains peanuts. His cousin was allergic — good to know before sharing.\n\n> In first period, the teacher handed out a one-page sheet about the co-op placement. Jaden skimmed it for the deadline — Friday — and circled it. In the hall, a poster said a bursary for students entering the trades was closing soon. He took a photo of it to read later.\n\n> At work, the opening checklist was taped to the wall: wipe counters, check fridge temperature, log it on the sheet, initial the sheet. Last month a new hire skipped the fridge log. When the inspector came, the gap in the record cost the store a warning. Jaden did not want to be that guy. He logged 3°C and initialled it.\n\n> At home, his mom asked him to read a letter from the housing office. The letter said the rent increase would begin on the first of next month, unless they replied within 14 days. Jaden found the date, found the phone number, and wrote the deadline on the fridge calendar.'
  };
  JADEN[4] = JADEN[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "He read it twice." The text is a one-line message from his supervisor. What does reading it twice imply about how Jaden treats a text that changes his day?\n- "Jaden did not want to be that guy." Which earlier sentence tells you who "that guy" is, and what did his mistake cost the store?\n- The housing letter gives 14 days to reply. Why does Jaden find the phone number as well as the date — what does that second step suggest about reading for a purpose?';
  const SKILLS = {
    1: '> **Why employers talk about "skills"** — Bosses want people who can learn, communicate and solve problems. Canada names nine **Skills for Success**. Reading and writing are two of them.\n\n> **Reading** — Reading means finding, understanding and using information. A plumber reads a parts list. A worker reads a safety sheet. They read for a **purpose**.\n\n> **Writing** — Writing means sharing information in words. Clear writing saves time.\n\n> **The warning** — Skills you do not use **fade**. Skills you practise **grow**.',
    2: '> **Why employers talk about "skills"** — When a hiring manager reads a résumé, they are not only looking for experience. They are looking for proof that a person can learn, communicate and solve problems. The Government of Canada\'s Skills for Success model names nine skills that people use in almost every job: adaptability, collaboration, communication, creativity and innovation, digital, numeracy, problem solving, reading and writing.\n\n> **Reading: more than sounding out words** — In this model, reading means being able to find, understand and use information that comes through words, symbols and images. A plumber reads a parts list. A personal support worker reads a care plan. A warehouse worker reads a safety data sheet. Each of them is reading for a purpose — and the purpose decides how they read. Nobody reads a parts list from top to bottom like a novel. They scan for the one line they need.\n\n> **Writing: sharing what you know** — Writing means sharing information using written words, symbols and images. On the job, that includes short texts to a supervisor, incident reports, order forms and e-mails to customers. Clear writing saves time; unclear writing creates mistakes that someone has to fix later.\n\n> **The cautionary part** — Skills that are not used fade. A person who avoids reading at work ends up depending on co-workers to tell them what a document says — and sooner or later, a co-worker is not there. The good news is that the opposite is also true: skills that are practised grow. Reading a little, often, with a purpose, is how most adults improve.'
  };
  SKILLS[4] = SKILLS[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "Nobody reads a parts list from top to bottom like a novel." Which approach from this lesson is the author describing, and why does the purpose make it the right one?\n- The heading "The cautionary part" comes last. How does the final sentence — "Reading a little, often, with a purpose" — turn the warning into advice?\n- "Sooner or later, a co-worker is not there." What does this phrase imply about depending on other people to read for you?';
  const BOUNCE = {
    1: '> Everyone gets knocked down. Getting back up is called **resilience**. You can learn it.\n\n> Resilient people **admit** the failure. Then they ask: "What can I control right now?"\n\n> They **stay connected**. Hiding makes the wall taller.\n\n> They keep a **routine**: sleep, breakfast, show up.\n\n> They remember hard times **end**. You have survived every bad day so far.\n\n> A setback is an **event**, not who you are.',
    2: '> Everyone gets knocked down. The difference between people who stay down and people who get back up is not luck, and it is not being born tough. Psychologists call the ability to recover from setbacks **resilience**, and the research is clear that it can be learned.\n\n> Resilient people do not pretend that a failure did not happen. Instead, they **acknowledge** it, feel the disappointment, and then ask a practical question: "What is one thing I can control right now?" A student who fails a test cannot change the mark. She can control whether she looks at which questions she got wrong. That small step is where a comeback starts.\n\n> A second habit is staying connected. People who hit a wall often want to hide — skip class, stop answering messages, throw in the towel. But **isolation** makes the wall taller. The American Psychological Association\'s advice for teens puts staying connected with friends, family and community first on its list of ways to build resilience.\n\n> Third, resilient people keep a routine. When life feels out of control, small regular habits — sleeping at the same time, eating breakfast, showing up — give the brain something solid to stand on. Routines are boring. That is the point.\n\n> Finally, resilient people remember that hard times end. One useful exercise is to list a challenge you have already survived. If you are reading this, you have survived every bad day you have ever had. The record is 100 per cent.\n\n> None of this means a setback does not hurt. It means that a setback is an event, not an **identity**. Failing a test is something that happened to you on a Tuesday. It is not who you are.'
  };
  BOUNCE[4] = BOUNCE[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "The record is 100 per cent." Which two sentences before it set up this line, and why is it placed at the end of the paragraph?\n- "Routines are boring. That is the point." Explain the contrast the author is drawing between boring and useful.\n- The passage ends with "on a Tuesday." What does naming an ordinary weekday do to the idea that a setback is an event, not an identity?';
  const MARCUS = {
    1: '> Marcus wanted his forklift licence. His uncle promised him a job if he passed. The test was Friday. On Thursday night, Marcus went to the lake with friends.\n\n> The test asked about charts and safety rules. Marcus did not know the answers. He got **14 out of 20**. The pass mark was **15**. He did not call his uncle.\n\n> His uncle called him. He said, "When is the next test? Come Sunday. Bring the manual."\n\n> On Sunday they read the manual together for two hours. Marcus saw that the chart was not hard. He had just never read it.\n\n> He passed the second test with **19 out of 20**.',
    2: '> Marcus had told everyone he was getting his forklift licence. His uncle had promised him a job at the depot the day he passed, and the pay was almost double what he made stocking shelves. The written test was on a Friday. On Thursday night, his friends were going to the lake.\n\n> "It\'s twenty questions," Marcus said, grabbing his jacket. "I\'ve driven the thing at my uncle\'s place a hundred times."\n\n> The test did not ask whether he could drive. It asked about load capacity charts, about the three-point stance for climbing on, about what to do when a load blocks your view. Marcus read the first question three times. He recognized the words. He did not know the answer. He left four questions blank and guessed on six.\n\n> The examiner slid the sheet back across the table without saying anything. Fourteen out of twenty. Fifteen was the pass. Marcus walked to the bus stop and did not call his uncle.\n\n> His uncle called him instead, that night. "So?" Marcus told him. There was a long silence, and then his uncle said, "Okay. When\'s the next sitting?" Marcus said he didn\'t know. His uncle said, "Find out. Then come by Sunday and bring the manual."\n\n> On Sunday they sat at the kitchen table with the manual open between them. His uncle did not lecture. He put his finger on the load capacity chart and said, "Read me this one." Marcus read it. "Now tell me what it means." Marcus tried, got it wrong, read it again, got it right. They did that for two hours. By the end Marcus noticed something: the chart was not hard. He had just never actually read it.\n\n> He passed the second sitting with nineteen out of twenty. The question he missed was about the three-point stance. He looked it up on the bus home.'
  };
  MARCUS[4] = MARCUS[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "He recognized the words. He did not know the answer." What is the difference between these two sentences, and why does the author place them side by side?\n- The examiner slides the sheet back "without saying anything," and later there is "a long silence" from the uncle. What does each silence suggest, and how are they different?\n- "He looked it up on the bus home." Compare this with "grabbing his jacket" on Thursday night. What has changed, and what evidence shows it?';
  const SCHEDULE ='| Program | Mon | Tue | Wed | Thu | Fri | Room | Cost |\n| Drop-in basketball (16+) | 7–9 pm | — | 7–9 pm | — | 6–9 pm | Gym | Free |\n| Homework help | 3:30–5 | 3:30–5 | 3:30–5 | 3:30–5 | — | Library | Free |\n| Résumé clinic | — | 4–6 pm | — | — | 4–6 pm | Room 104 | Free |\n| Forklift safety (cert.) | — | — | 6–9 pm | — | — | Room 210 | $45 |\n| Food handler course | — | — | — | 5–9 pm | — | Kitchen | $30 |\n| Open gym | — | 7–9 pm | — | 7–9 pm | — | Gym | Free |';
