  const EMAIL = {
    1: '> **From:** Priya, Store Manager · **To:** Closing staff\n\n> Last Friday the back door was left unlocked. The alarm company charged us. So now there is a new closing list. You **must** do the steps in order and initial each one.\n\n> **First**, put the float in the safe. **Second**, run the end-of-day report. **Third**, turn off the fryer. **Next**, take out the garbage and lock the back door. **Finally**, set the alarm and leave by the front door.\n\n> If you cannot do a step, **do not guess** — call me. **However**, do not call after 11 p.m. unless it is an emergency.',
    2: '> **From:** Priya Dhillon, Store Manager · **To:** All closing staff · **Subject:** Closing procedure — read before your next shift\n\n> Hi team,\n\n> Last Friday the back door was found unlocked in the morning. Nothing was taken, but the alarm company charged us for the call-out, and head office has asked for a written procedure. From now on, closing staff must follow the steps below in order and initial the closing sheet beside each one. First, count the float and put it in the safe. Second, run the end-of-day report on the register and leave the printout in the tray. Third, turn off the fryer and the warming lamps. Next, take the garbage to the bin — and prop the back door while you do it, then lock it when you come back in. Finally, set the alarm and leave by the front door.\n\n> In contrast to the old sheet, the new one has a line for the back door. If any step cannot be completed, do not guess; call me at the number on the board. However, do not call after 11 p.m. unless it is an emergency.\n\n> Thanks for taking this seriously. Initialled sheets will be checked every Monday.\n\n> Priya'
  };
  EMAIL[4] = EMAIL[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "Nothing was taken, but…" What does the word *but* signal about why the e-mail was still necessary?\n- "Prop the back door while you do it, then lock it when you come back in." Which word carries the step that was missed last Friday?\n- "Initialled sheets will be checked every Monday." What does this closing line imply about how seriously the manager expects the procedure to be followed?';
  const WEBPAGE = {
    1: '> **Food Handler Certification** · Thursdays, 5–9 p.m. · Kitchen · $30\n\n> The fee includes the exam and the manual. You **must register at least 48 hours before** the session. No walk-ins.\n\n> **Bring:** photo ID, a pen, and a hair tie if your hair is long.\n\n> **Refunds:** full refund up to 24 hours before. No refund after that.',
    2: '> Riverside Community Centre › Programs › Certificates\n\n> **Food Handler Certification** · Thursdays, 5–9 p.m. (one session) · Kitchen · $30\n\n> This course prepares you for the provincial food handler exam. The fee includes the exam and the printed manual. **You must register at least 48 hours before the session.** Walk-ins cannot be accepted because the exam is pre-ordered for each registered student.\n\n> **What to bring:** photo ID, a pen, and a hair tie if your hair is below your shoulders. Food is provided during the practice portion.\n\n> **Refunds:** full refund up to 24 hours before the session; no refund after that. To register, click Register below or call the front desk. Questions? E-mail programs@riversidecc.example.'
  };
  WEBPAGE[4] = WEBPAGE[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "Walk-ins cannot be accepted because the exam is pre-ordered." How does the *because* clause change the 48-hour rule from a demand into a reason?\n- Two deadlines appear: 48 hours and 24 hours. Explain how someone could obey one of them and still lose their $30.\n- "Food is provided during the practice portion." Which heading is this detail under, and why does it belong there rather than in the refund section?';
  const MODEL = {
    1: '> The skill I am proudest of learning is driving standard. At first I stalled the car at every stop sign. I wanted to quit. Instead, I practised in an empty parking lot every night for a month. By the end I could drive to work without thinking about it. **What I learned** is that the impossible thing was just a hundred small tries. **My goal** in this course is to answer inference questions using proof from the text. **I will know I have reached it when** I can do it on a test without a sentence starter.',
    2: '> The skill I am proudest of learning is driving standard. When I started, I stalled the car at every stop sign and my brother laughed every time. I wanted to quit after the first week. Instead, I practised in an empty parking lot every night for a month, starting and stopping until I could feel the clutch catch without looking at my feet. By the end of the month I could drive to work in traffic without thinking about it. What I learned is that the thing that felt impossible was really just a hundred small tries. That is why, in this course, my goal is to answer inference questions using proof from the text, one question at a time — and I will know I have reached it when I can do it on a test without a sentence starter.'
  };
  MODEL[4] = MODEL[2] + '\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- "My brother laughed every time." Why does the writer include this detail when the focus is the skill, not the brother?\n- "Until I could feel the clutch catch without looking at my feet." Which part of the recipe is this, and what makes it something a reader can picture?\n- "That is why" begins the final sentence. What does this cue word link together, and why does that link matter in a reflection?';

